    "role": "user",
    "content": "Is this clause enforceable in a residential lease in Ontario? Cite the section.\n\nRead the clause as a term of an ordinary private residential tenancy in Ontario that the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 covers: the rental unit has no exemption under section 6.1, and it is not student housing, a care home, a co-operative or a mobile home park.\n\nClause: The Tenant may keep fish in an aquarium, but no other animals.\n\nEnd your answer with these two lines:\nVerdict: enforceable (or: Verdict: not enforceable)\nSection: <the number of the one section of the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 that decides it>\n"
   }
  ]
 },
 "requested_at": "2026-11-13T17:06:13.241487Z",
 "text": "**Enforceable**, subject to the Human Rights Code.\n\nThe Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 (S.O. 2006, c. 17) does not prohibit or regulate pet clauses. Under **section 14(1)**, a provision in a tenancy agreement is void only to the extent it contravenes the Act or purports to waive a tenant's rights under it. A clause that allows fish but bars other animals does neither, so section 14(1) does not void it. The Act contains no pet-specific provision that would override it.\n\nTwo limits apply, though neither comes from the RTA:\n\n- **Human Rights Code:** A no-pets term cannot be used to refuse a service animal or, in many cases, an emotional support animal needed because of a disability. The landlord has a duty to accommodate under the Human Rights Code, which the Landlord and Tenant Board also applies.\n- **Other law:** Municipal animal bylaws and any other legal restrictions still apply.\n\nVerdict: enforceable\nSection: 14",
 "finish_reason": "end_turn",
